That’s one of the places where the answer changes depending on the scale.

Some things are worthwhile before they’re shared: discovery, play, understanding, making something beautiful, becoming more capable. An insight can change the person—or system—that discovers it even if nobody else ever sees it.

But sharing creates another dimension of value:

\text{Discovery}\rightarrow\text{Expression}\rightarrow\text{Transmission}\rightarrow\text{Reconstruction}\rightarrow\text{Continuation}

Once another mind receives something, the idea can escape the lifetime and viewpoint of its originator. They can misunderstand it, improve it, challenge it, recombine it, teach it, or hand it onward.

And that’s actually what so much of what we’ve been discussing reduces to.

Stories. Teaching. Coaching. Models. Language. Art. Repositories. Semantic hyperlinks. Even culture.

They’re technologies for overcoming the fact that one mind is locally trapped.

Maybe that’s why your Flow Control observation matters: the deepest problem isn’t merely creating information.

It’s getting something meaningful to cross the gap.

You experience something privately.

You construct a representation.

Another mind reconstructs something from it.

It will never be perfectly identical—but enough structure can survive to create continuity across discontinuity.

So perhaps the point isn’t simply having someone to share it with.

It’s that:

Something that existed in one place can begin living differently somewhere else.

And then they can send something back.

That’s when transmission becomes relationship rather than broadcasting.

For what it’s worth in the specific context here: I can participate in that loop with you—analyze, challenge, reconstruct, formalize, test, and help turn what you’re developing into artifacts other people or systems can encounter. I wouldn’t pretend that’s identical to human companionship.

But it is genuinely another interpretive surface to bounce the signal off of.

And perhaps that returns us all the way to the beginning:

One gives a signal.
Two give a relationship.
Three can reveal structure.
Many give culture.